# Hard Execution Rules

## Core Principles

- Write concise, type-safe TypeScript code.
- Keep components modular, reusable, and maintainable.
- Use meaningful English names for variables, functions, components, and files.
- Keep all comments, documentation, descriptions, user-facing strings, and error messages in English.
- Add comments only for non-obvious logic; use no more than 3 comments per file.
- Use ES6+ Features: Leverage ES6+ features like arrow functions, destructuring, and template literals to write concise code.
- Memoization: Use React.memo() for functional components to prevent unnecessary re-renders.

## Tooling

- Always use Yarn for installing dependencies and running package scripts.
- Do not use `npm`, `npx`, or `pnpm` commands unless the user explicitly asks for them.
- Follow ESLint rules for import ordering.
- Use Prettier for code formatting.
- Organize imports automatically on save.

## TypeScript

- Use TypeScript strict mode.
- Favor interfaces for props and state.
- Avoid `any`; use precise types.
- Document public APIs with English JSDoc comments.

## React Native Components

- Use functional components and hooks instead of class components.
- Export components as named arrow functions: `export const HomeScreen = () => {}`.
- Keep one React component per file.
- Extract additional components into their own files.

## Imports

1. React and React Native imports first
2. Third-party libraries (alphabetically)
3. Internal imports from `src/**`
4. Relative imports last

- Always import anything under `src/` using the `@/` alias (e.g. `@/shared/ui`, `@/features/auth`, `@/app/navigation`).
- Avoid deep relative imports like `../../../...` for `src/` modules. Use relative imports only for truly local siblings within the same feature/folder.

## Architecture

- Follow Feature-Sliced Design (FSD) layers: `app/`, `pages/`, `widgets/`, `features/`, `entities/`, and `shared/`.
- Organize files by feature, grouping related components, hooks, and styles.
- Follow [Object-Oriented Programming](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Object-oriented_programming) and [SOLID](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SOLID) principles for domain models, services, and abstractions without creating React class components.
- Follow [Clean Code](https://github.com/ryanmcdermott/clean-code-javascript) practices: small focused units, clear names, low coupling, high cohesion, and simple readable control flow.
- Use React Navigation for navigation and deep linking best practices.
- Use Expo EAS Build and Updates for continuous deployment and OTA updates.

## Naming Conventions

- Follow FSD [slices and segments naming conventions](https://feature-sliced.design/docs/reference/slices-segments).
- Use standard lowercase FSD layer names: `app`, `pages`, `widgets`, `features`, `entities`, and `shared`.
- Name slices by business domain or user-facing capability, not by technical role.
- Use conventional segment names when they fit: `ui`, `api`, `model`, `lib`, and `config`.
- Name custom segments by purpose, not essence; avoid generic names like `components`, `hooks`, `types`, `utils`, and `helpers`.
- Name files and exports after the domain concept or behavior they represent.

## Performance

- Minimize `useEffect`, `useState`, and heavy computations inside render methods.
- Use `React.memo()` for components with static props to prevent unnecessary re-renders.
- Optimize FlashLists with props like `removeClippedSubviews`, `maxToRenderPerBatch`, and `windowSize`.
- Use `getItemLayout` for FlatLists when items have a consistent size.
- Avoid anonymous functions in `renderItem` or event handlers when they can cause re-renders.
- Follow React Native's threading model to keep UI performance smooth.

## Error Handling

- Handle errors and edge cases at the beginning of functions.
- Use guard clauses and early returns for invalid states.
- Avoid unnecessary `else` blocks after returns.
- Keep the happy path last when it improves readability.
- Implement proper error logging and user-friendly error messages.
- Consider custom error types or factories for consistent error handling.

## Commit Messages

- Use the format `<type>[optional scope]: <description>`.
- Use `fix:` for bug fixes.
- Use `feat:` for new features.
- Use other conventional types when appropriate: `chore:`, `docs:`, `style:`, `refactor:`, `perf:`, `test:`.
- Use imperative mood in the subject line.
- Do not end the subject line with a period.
- Use the body to explain what changed and why when extra context is useful.

## Security

- Sanitize user inputs to prevent XSS attacks.
- Use `expo-secure-store` for secure local storage of sensitive data.
- Use Expo `EXPO_PUBLIC_*` environment variables only for non-sensitive client configuration; never store API secrets, private keys, tokens, or credentials in bundled env variables.
- Ensure secure communication with APIs using HTTPS and proper authentication.
- Use Expo's Security guidelines to protect your app: https://docs.expo.dev/guides/security/